include "../../linux.mereo"
include "../../core.mereo"

-- A CHECKED access in a loop whose bound is a scalar rather than the span's own
-- length. That shape is what the bound hoist is for.
--
-- `span.at` tests `offset < length`, and after splicing that is a LOAD from the
-- view's bytes -- so without the hoist the bound is fetched from memory on every
-- pass, and a memory read in an exit test stops the loop vectorising. Measured
-- on this program: 4 vector instructions with the load in the loop, 41 with it
-- hoisted. The check is still here, and `tests/progs/views_at_past_end` pins
-- that it still fires.
--
-- The hoist does not tell GCC anything it could not work out. Every small
-- reproduction of this shape -- the punned byte array, the local buffer filled
-- by a syscall, the early exit into a block with side effects -- lifts the load
-- and vectorises with no help at all. Something between those and the whole
-- program stops it, and it is not aliasing: `-fstrict-aliasing` changes neither
-- column. What the hoist supplies is a form, not a fact.
--
-- This program is the only one in the tree the hoist changes -- 89 shipped
-- binaries and 78 test binaries are byte-identical with it off. Nothing gates
-- the 41 either; the black-box case below pins the OUTPUT, which is the same
-- both ways. See `todo.md`.
--
-- Note the loop is bounded by `count`, NOT by `v.length`. Where they are the
-- same name GCC proves the check redundant and deletes it, and the hoist stands
-- back rather than breaking that -- `tests/versus/cases/index_safe` is that
-- case, and it reports EXTRA SYSCALLS if the hoist stops standing back.
--
--   echo -n abc | span_hoist   ->  294        (97 + 98 + 99)

program goes
  block is 4096 bytes
  count is 0
  i is 0
  b is 0
  total is 0
  room is 64 bytes

  page is already builder (data is room, count is 0, limit is 64)

  input is already linux.file (descriptor is 0)

  input.read (buffer is block, capacity is 4096, count is count)

  v is already span (data is block, length is count)

  step goes
    leave step when i >= count
    v.at (offset is i, byte is b)
    total is total + b
    i is i + 1
    repeat step
  end

  page.number (value is total)
  page.byte (value is 10)

  screen is already linux.file (descriptor is 1)

  screen.write (buffer is page.data, count is page.count)

end
